\section{The \texttt{users} subcommand group}
\label{sec:users}

User administration is the primary motivation for this tool, and a
typical workflow chains three related questions: who is on the
server, who has been invited but not yet joined, and how do I invite
someone new?
Grouping those verbs under a single [[users]] command family keeps
the top-level namespace tidy and makes the relationship obvious from
the help output.
The three flat verbs that follow ([[send]], [[streams]], [[topics]],
[[read]]) each speak to a different Zulip concept and do not gain
anything from further nesting.

We declare the group up-front so each per-verb handler below can
attach itself via [[@users_app.command(...)]] as it is introduced.
Setting [[no_args_is_help=True]] makes [[zulipcli users]] print the
subcommand list rather than failing silently---useful both for
discovery and as a migration signal for anyone still typing the flat
[[zulipcli users PATTERN]] form that this tool used to accept.

<<functions>>=
users_app = typer.Typer(
    help="List, invite, and inspect users on the server.",
    no_args_is_help=True,
)
@

Zulip's invitation API identifies roles by integer codes, and the
same codes come back when we list pending invitations.
Defining the label$\leftrightarrow$code mapping once, next to the
commands that consume it, means the rest of the code never has to
touch the raw integers---and the two tables stay in sync because the
reverse lookup is derived from the forward one.

<<constants>>=
_INVITE_ROLE_CODES = {
    "owner": 100,
    "admin": 200,
    "moderator": 300,
    "member": 400,
    "guest": 600,
}
_INVITE_ROLE_LABELS = {
    code: label for label, code in _INVITE_ROLE_CODES.items()
}
@


\subsection{Listing users: \texttt{users list}}

The Zulip API's [[get_users]] method returns every user with fields
like [[full_name]], [[email]], [[delivery_email]], [[user_id]],
[[is_bot]], and [[is_active]].
The library also exposes a [[get_members]] alias that simply forwards
to [[get_users]]; it is kept around for backwards compatibility, and
its docstring already warns that a deprecation warning may be added
later.
We call the canonical [[get_users]] so the code keeps working when
that warning lands.

The response shape still uses [[members]] as its top-level key,
because the server's JSON format predates the client-side rename and
cannot change without breaking every existing client.
That is why the rest of this file reads [[result["members"]]] even
though the call is [[get_users]].

The [[email]] field is the address Zulip uses for API routing
(often [[user42@subdomain.zulipchat.com]] when the realm hides real
addresses), whereas [[delivery_email]] is the mailbox an operator
actually wants to reach.
When the caller is an organisation admin and the realm's policy
permits it, [[delivery_email]] carries the user's real email;
otherwise Zulip returns [[None]].

Showing both columns lets scripts correlate a Zulip identity with a
real inbox in one shot, which is the whole reason this command
exists.
We print tab-separated lines so [[grep]], [[cut]], and [[sort]]
continue to work, and we print an empty field when Zulip withheld
[[delivery_email]]---that keeps the column count stable for
[[cut -f3]] consumers.
By default we exclude bots and deactivated users because the typical
question is \enquote{who, on my team, can I reach right now?}.

<<functions>>=
@users_app.command("list")
def _users_list_handler(
    ctx: typer.Context,
    pattern: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Argument(
            help="filter users by name, email, or delivery "
                 "email (regex)",
        ),
    ] = None,
    include_bots: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(help="include bot accounts in output"),
    ] = False,
    include_deactivated: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(help="include deactivated accounts in output"),
    ] = False,
):
    """List users on the Zulip server."""
    users_list_command(
        _get_client_from_context(ctx),
        SimpleNamespace(
            pattern=pattern,
            include_bots=include_bots,
            include_deactivated=include_deactivated,
        ),
    )
@

The implementation fetches all members, applies the filters, and
prints each matching user.
We compile the pattern once (if given) and match it
case-insensitively against the full name, Zulip email, and delivery
email, so [[zulipcli users list bosk]] finds the user whether
\enquote{bosk} appears in their display name, their Zulip handle, or
their real mailbox.

<<functions>>=
def users_list_command(client, args):
    """List users, optionally filtered by pattern."""
    result = _require_success(client.get_users())

    if args.pattern:
        pattern = re.compile(args.pattern, re.IGNORECASE)
    else:
        pattern = None

    for member in sorted(result["members"],
                         key=lambda m: m["full_name"]):
        <<skip filtered users>>
        delivery = member.get("delivery_email") or ""
        print(
            f"{member['full_name']}\t{member['email']}\t{delivery}"
        )
@

We skip users that don't match the active filters.
Order matters: cheap boolean checks first, then the regex.
The pattern search ORs three haystacks so a match in any column
passes; when [[delivery_email]] is absent or empty we simply exclude
it from the haystack list rather than trying to match an empty
string.

<<skip filtered users>>=
if not args.include_bots and member.get("is_bot", False):
    continue
if not args.include_deactivated \
        and not member.get("is_active", True):
    continue
if pattern:
    haystacks = [
        member["full_name"],
        member["email"],
        member.get("delivery_email") or "",
    ]
    if not any(pattern.search(field) for field in haystacks if field):
        continue
@

