\subsection{Listing pending invitations: \texttt{users invited}}

Pending invitations are the mirror image of [[users list]]: email
addresses that have received an invitation link but not yet redeemed
it.
The [[zulip]] Python library exposes no dedicated invitation method,
so we reach for the escape hatch that the library itself uses
internally when a helper is missing:
[[client.call_endpoint(url="invites", method="GET")]].

The API requires organisation-administrator privileges, so a
non-admin caller will see [[Error: Must be an organization
administrator]] on stderr rather than a traceback; that path reuses
our existing [[_require_success]] helper unchanged.

Each invitation record carries an [[email]], an [[expiry_date]]
(Unix seconds, or [[None]] for never-expiring links), an
[[invited_as]] role code, and an [[invited_by_user_id]].
We format each row as four tab-separated columns, translate the
expiry to ISO-8601 UTC, and map the role code back to its label so
scripts never have to memorise Zulip's integers.
An empty invitation list prints nothing, matching the silent
\enquote{no results} convention already used by [[users list]].

<<functions>>=
@users_app.command("invited")
def _users_invited_handler(
    ctx: typer.Context,
    pattern: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Argument(
            help="filter invitations by invitee email (regex)",
        ),
    ] = None,
):
    """List pending (unaccepted) invitations."""
    users_invited_command(
        _get_client_from_context(ctx),
        SimpleNamespace(pattern=pattern),
    )
@

<<functions>>=
def users_invited_command(client, args):
    """List pending invitations, optionally filtered by email."""
    result = _require_success(client.call_endpoint(
        url="invites",
        method="GET",
    ))

    if args.pattern:
        pattern = re.compile(args.pattern, re.IGNORECASE)
    else:
        pattern = None

    for invite in sorted(result.get("invites", []),
                         key=lambda inv: inv["email"]):
        if pattern and not pattern.search(invite["email"]):
            continue
        print(_format_invitation_row(invite))
@

Expiry formatting has two shapes to handle.
[[None]] represents a never-expiring invitation and prints the empty
string so downstream [[cut -f]] callers still see four fields; an
integer is a Unix timestamp and renders as UTC ISO-8601 to match the
format already produced by the [[read]] command.

<<functions>>=
def _format_invitation_row(invite):
    """Format one invitation as a tab-separated row."""
    expiry = invite.get("expiry_date")
    if expiry is None:
        expiry_iso = ""
    else:
        expiry_iso = datetime.fromtimestamp(
            expiry,
            tz=timezone.utc,
        ).isoformat(timespec="seconds")
    role_code = invite.get("invited_as")
    role_label = _INVITE_ROLE_LABELS.get(role_code, str(role_code))
    inviter = invite.get("invited_by_user_id", "")
    return f"{invite['email']}\t{expiry_iso}\t{role_label}\t{inviter}"
@

