\section{The \texttt{send} subcommand}

The [[send]] subcommand wraps [[client.send_message()]].
It supports two modes: stream messages (requiring \texttt{-{}-stream}
and \texttt{-{}-topic}) and direct messages (requiring \texttt{-{}-to}).

We accept the message body either as a positional argument or from
standard input.
Reading from stdin is useful for piping output from other commands
directly into a Zulip message, which is a common scripting pattern.

The [[--to]] value is a regex matched against every active non-bot
user's full name, Zulip email, and delivery email; all matches
receive the message.
This turns \enquote{DM everyone in the Alice team} into a one-liner
instead of forcing the caller to copy--paste addresses, and makes a
single-address send work the same way (any valid email is itself a
regex that happens to match exactly one user).
Because a loose pattern can reach more people than intended, a
multi-match send pauses for confirmation on an interactive terminal
and requires [[--yes]] on a pipe.

<<functions>>=
def _send_handler(
    ctx: typer.Context,
    stream: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Option(
            "--stream",
            "-s",
            help="destination stream (channel) name",
            autocompletion=complete_stream_names,
        ),
    ] = None,
    topic: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Option(
            "--topic",
            "-t",
            help="topic within the stream",
            autocompletion=complete_topic_names,
        ),
    ] = None,
    to: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Option(
            help="direct-message recipient: regex matched against "
                 "full name, Zulip email, and delivery email "
                 "(all matches receive the message)",
            autocompletion=complete_user_emails,
        ),
    ] = None,
    yes: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--yes",
            "-y",
            help="skip confirmation when --to matches "
                 "multiple users",
        ),
    ] = False,
    message: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Argument(help="message content (reads stdin if omitted)"),
    ] = None,
):
    """Send a message."""
    send_command(
        _get_client_from_context(ctx),
        SimpleNamespace(
            stream=stream,
            topic=topic,
            to=to,
            yes=yes,
            message=message,
        ),
    )
@

The Zulip API distinguishes between [[stream]] messages (sent to a
channel and topic) and [[direct]] messages (sent to one or more users
by email).
We require exactly one of these two modes and report an error if the
user provides neither or both.

<<functions>>=
def send_command(client, args):
    """Send a message to a stream or directly to one or more users."""
    content = args.message
    if content is None:
        content = sys.stdin.read()

    if args.stream and args.to:
        _exit_with_error("specify either --stream/--topic or --to")

    if args.stream:
        <<send stream message>>
    elif args.to:
        <<send direct message>>
    else:
        _exit_with_error("specify --stream/--topic or --to")
@

For stream messages, a topic is required---Zulip enforces this on the
server side, but we catch it early to give a clearer error message.

<<send stream message>>=
if not args.topic:
    _exit_with_error("--topic is required with --stream")
request = {
    "type": "stream",
    "to": args.stream,
    "topic": args.topic,
    "content": content,
}
_require_success(client.send_message(request))
@

Direct messages use the [[direct]] type (called [[private]] in older
API versions).
The [[to]] field on the wire accepts a list of Zulip emails; our
resolver turns the caller's regex into that list and asks for
confirmation when more than one user is about to be messaged.
A match on [[delivery_email]] still sends to the matched user's Zulip
[[email]], because the API expects the registered address rather than
the user's mailbox.

<<send direct message>>=
recipients = _resolve_direct_recipients(client, args.to)
if not recipients:
    _exit_with_error(
        f"--to pattern {args.to!r} matched no active users"
    )
if len(recipients) > 1 and not _confirm_recipients(
    recipients,
    assume_yes=args.yes,
):
    _exit_with_error("aborted by user")

request = {
    "type": "direct",
    "to": [member["email"] for member in recipients],
    "content": content,
}
_require_success(client.send_message(request))
@

We factor the resolution into its own helper for two reasons.
First, the regex semantics already drive the help text of [[--to]]
and the completion helper, and collecting the logic in one place
keeps all three aligned.
Second, tests for the fan-out behaviour can then drive this helper
directly without having to fake a message body or a confirmation
prompt.

Deactivated accounts are excluded because sending to a disabled user
is always a mistake.
Bots are \emph{not} excluded: Zulip treats bot DMs as a first-class
feature---reminder bots, Q\&A bots, and webhook bots all rely on
humans being able to message them directly.
If the regex matches a bot, the bot should receive the DM just like
any other recipient.

<<functions>>=
def _resolve_direct_recipients(client, pattern_str):
    """Return active members whose fields match the regex."""
    result = _require_success(client.get_users())
    pattern = re.compile(pattern_str, re.IGNORECASE)
    matches = []
    for member in sorted(result["members"],
                         key=lambda m: m["full_name"]):
        if not member.get("is_active", True):
            continue
        haystacks = [
            member["full_name"],
            member["email"],
            member.get("delivery_email") or "",
        ]
        if any(pattern.search(field) for field in haystacks if field):
            matches.append(member)
    return matches
@

The confirmation helper prints the matched recipients to stderr so
the preview does not pollute stdout (which scripts may be capturing),
then asks on stdin.
A non-interactive stdin without [[--yes]] refuses rather than silently
confirming: fan-out on a pipe is exactly the case where an accidental
broadcast is most expensive to undo.

<<functions>>=
def _confirm_recipients(members, *, assume_yes):
    """Confirm sending a DM to multiple recipients."""
    for member in members:
        print(
            f"{member['full_name']}\t{member['email']}",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
    if assume_yes:
        return True
    stdin_is_tty = getattr(sys.stdin, "isatty", None)
    if not stdin_is_tty or not stdin_is_tty():
        _exit_with_error(
            f"--to matched {len(members)} recipients; "
            "pass --yes to send without confirmation"
        )
    answer = input(f"Send to {len(members)} users? [y/N] ")
    return answer.strip().lower() in {"y", "yes"}
@

